Title: A Christmas Carol
Credit: Written by
Author: Charles Dickens
Source: Adapted from the book by Charles Dickens
Draft date: 12/15/2024
Notes: Medium = photoreal live-action Victorian period drama; naturalistic faces, fabric, fog, and candlelight; no illustration or animation.

FADE IN:

EXT. LONDON STREETS AND COURT ON CHRISTMAS EVE - DAY

Dense brown fog. Cold, bleak, biting weather. People wheeze past, beating hands upon their breasts, stamping feet on pavement stones. Candles flare in neighbouring office windows like ruddy smears upon the palpable brown air. Above a warehouse door the sign reads: SCROOGE AND MARLEY.

(SOUND: muffled church bells, distant coughing, boots on wet stone)

INT. SCROOGE AND MARLEY'S COUNTING-HOUSE - DAY

A cramped City office. Tiny mean fire in the grate. EBENEZER SCROOGE, elderly, thin, pointed nose, shrivelled cheek, stiffened gait, red-rimmed eyes, thin blue-tinged lips, frosty white rime on head, eyebrows and wiry chin, sits busy at his desk in dark severe day clothes. Through an open door, BOB CRATCHIT, thin, threadbare clerk's clothes, long white comforter dangling, copies letters in the dismal little cell beyond—the Tank—where the fire looks like one coal.

(SOUND: scratch of quills, fog-muffled street noise, one coal settling)

The door opens. FRED, young, ruddy handsome face, sparkling eyes, glowing from rapid walking in fog and frost, bursts in.

FRED
A merry Christmas, uncle! God save you!

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Christmas a humbug, uncle! You don't mean that, I am sure?

SCROOGE
I do. Merry Christmas! What right have you to be merry? You're poor enough.

FRED
What right have you to be dismal? You're rich enough.

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Don't be cross, uncle!

SCROOGE
What else can I be, when I live in such a world of fools as this? Merry Christmas! Out upon merry Christmas!

SCROOGE
What's Christmas time to you but a time for paying bills without money; a time for finding yourself a year older, but not an hour richer?

SCROOGE
If I could work my will, every idiot who goes about with Merry Christmas on his lips should be boiled with his own pudding, and buried with a stake of holly through his heart!

FRED
Uncle!

SCROOGE
Nephew! Keep Christmas in your own way, and let me keep it in mine.

FRED
But you don't keep it.

SCROOGE
Let me leave it alone, then. Much good may it do you! Much good it has ever done you!

FRED
I am sure I have always thought of Christmas time as a good time; a kind, forgiving, charitable, pleasant time.

FRED
The only time I know of when men and women open their shut-up hearts freely and think of people below them as fellow-passengers to the grave.

FRED
And therefore, uncle, though it has never put a scrap of gold or silver in my pocket, I believe that it has done me good, and will do me good; and I say, God bless it!

In the Tank, Bob involuntarily applauds—then, sensible of the impropriety, pokes the fire and extinguishes the last frail spark.

SCROOGE
Let me hear another sound from you, and you'll keep your Christmas by losing your situation!

SCROOGE
You're quite a powerful speaker, sir. I wonder you don't go into Parliament.

FRED
Don't be angry, uncle. Come! Dine with us to-morrow.

Scrooge says he will see him—yes, indeed he does—in that extremity first.

FRED
But why?

SCROOGE
Why did you get married?

FRED
Because I fell in love.

SCROOGE
Because you fell in love! Good afternoon!

FRED
Nay, uncle, but you never came to see me before that happened. Why give it as a reason for not coming now?

SCROOGE
Good afternoon.

FRED
I want nothing from you; I ask nothing of you; why cannot we be friends?

SCROOGE
Good afternoon.

FRED
I am sorry, with all my heart, to find you so resolute. A Merry Christmas, uncle!

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

FRED
And A Happy New Year!

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

Fred leaves without an angry word. At the outer door he bestows the greetings of the season on Bob, who returns them cordially.

SCROOGE
(muttering)
There's another fellow, my clerk, with fifteen shillings a week, and a wife and family, talking about a merry Christmas. I'll retire to Bedlam.

Two PORTLY GENTLEMEN, pleasant to behold, hats off, books and papers in hand, enter and bow.

PORTLY GENTLEMAN 1
Scrooge and Marley's, I believe. Have I the pleasure of addressing Mr. Scrooge, or Mr. Marley?

SCROOGE
Mr. Marley has been dead these seven years. He died seven years ago, this very night.

PORTLY GENTLEMAN 1
We have no doubt his liberality is well represented by his surviving partner.

At the word "liberality," Scrooge frowns, shakes his head, hands the credentials back.

PORTLY GENTLEMAN 1
At this festive season of the year, Mr. Scrooge, it is more than usually desirable that we should make some slight provision for the Poor and destitute, who suffer greatly at the present time.

PORTLY GENTLEMAN 1
Many thousands are in want of common necessaries; hundreds of thousands are in want of common comforts, sir.

SCROOGE
Are there no prisons?

PORTLY GENTLEMAN 1
Plenty of prisons.

SCROOGE
And the Union workhouses? Are they still in operation?

PORTLY GENTLEMAN 1
They are. Still. I wish I could say they were not.

SCROOGE
The Treadmill and the Poor Law are in full vigour, then?

PORTLY GENTLEMAN 1
Both very busy, sir.

SCROOGE
Oh! I was afraid, from what you said at first, that something had occurred to stop them in their useful course. I'm very glad to hear it.

PORTLY GENTLEMAN 1
A few of us are endeavouring to raise a fund to buy the Poor some meat and drink, and means of warmth.

PORTLY GENTLEMAN 1
We choose this time, because it is a time, of all others, when Want is keenly felt, and Abundance rejoices. What shall I put you down for?

SCROOGE
Nothing!

PORTLY GENTLEMAN 1
You wish to be anonymous?

SCROOGE
I wish to be left alone. Since you ask me what I wish, gentlemen, that is my answer.

SCROOGE
I don't make merry myself at Christmas and I can't afford to make idle people merry.

SCROOGE
I help to support the establishments I have mentioned—they cost enough; and those who are badly off must go there.

PORTLY GENTLEMAN 2
Many can't go there; and many would rather die.

SCROOGE
If they would rather die, they had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

SCROOGE
Besides—excuse me—I don't know that.

PORTLY GENTLEMAN 1
But you might know it.

SCROOGE
It's not my business. It's enough for a man to understand his own business, and not to interfere with other people's. Mine occupies me constantly. Good afternoon, gentlemen!

The gentlemen withdraw. Scrooge resumes his labours with an improved opinion of himself.

(SOUND: fog thickening outside, church tower bell striking quarters)

EXT. LONDON STREETS AND COURT ON CHRISTMAS EVE - NIGHT

Foggier yet, and colder. Piercing, searching, biting cold. A scant young nose stoops at Scrooge's keyhole to regale him with a carol.

CAROL SINGER (O.S.)
God bless you, merry gentleman! May nothing you dismay!

Scrooge seizes the ruler. The singer flees in terror, leaving the keyhole to the fog and frost.

(SOUND: ruler clatter, fleeing footsteps, wind)

INT. SCROOGE AND MARLEY'S COUNTING-HOUSE - NIGHT

Hour of shutting up. Scrooge dismounts from his stool with an ill-will. Bob snuffs his candle and puts on his hat.

SCROOGE
You'll want all day to-morrow, I suppose?

BOB CRATCHIT
If quite convenient, sir.

SCROOGE
It's not convenient, and it's not fair. If I was to stop half-a-crown for it, you'd think yourself ill-used, I'll be bound?

Bob smiles faintly.

SCROOGE
And yet you don't think me ill-used, when I pay a day's wages for no work.

BOB CRATCHIT
It's only once a year, sir.

SCROOGE
A poor excuse for picking a man's pocket every twenty-fifth of December!

Scrooge buttons his great-coat to the chin.

SCROOGE
But I suppose you must have the whole day. Be here all the earlier next morning.

Bob promises. Scrooge walks out with a growl. The office closes in a twinkling. Bob, long ends of white comforter dangling (no great-coat), goes down a slide on Cornhill at the end of a lane of boys, twenty times, then runs home to Camden Town as hard as he can pelt.

(SOUND: scrape of ice slide, boys' shouts, Scrooge's growl)

EXT. LONDON STREETS AND COURT ON CHRISTMAS EVE - NIGHT

Scrooge takes his melancholy dinner in his usual melancholy tavern, reads newspapers, beguiles the evening with his banker's-book, then walks home through the fog.

(SOUND: tavern clatter fading, solitary footsteps on wet stone)

EXT. SCROOGE'S GLOOMY CHAMBERS - NIGHT

A lowering pile of building up a yard. Dark yard; even Scrooge gropes. Fog and frost hang about the black old gateway. The door knocker is very large—nothing particular about it.

Scrooge puts his key in the lock—and sees in the knocker, without intermediate change, not a knocker, but MARLEY'S FACE: dismal light like a bad lobster in a dark cellar; ghostly spectacles turned up on ghostly forehead; hair curiously stirred; eyes wide open, motionless; livid colour.

(SOUND: key scrape, sudden silence, distant drip)

As Scrooge looks fixedly, it is a knocker again. He is startled; his blood knows a terrible sensation. He turns the key sturdily, walks in, lights his candle. He pauses, looks cautiously behind the door—nothing but screws and nuts. He says "Pooh, pooh!" and closes it with a bang.

(SOUND: door bang like thunder, echoes through house and cellars)

INT. SCROOGE'S GLOOMY CHAMBERS - NIGHT

Wide dark stairs. Scrooge trims his candle as he goes up, not caring a button for the dark. Before he shuts his heavy door he walks through sitting-room, bedroom, lumber-room: nobody under table, sofa, bed, closet; small fire; spoon and basin; little saucepan of gruel upon the hob; old fire-guard, old shoes, two fish-baskets, washing-stand on three legs, poker.

Quite satisfied, he closes his door and double-locks himself in—not his custom. He takes off his cravat, puts on dressing-gown, slippers and nightcap, sits before the low fire to take his gruel. The old fireplace is paved with quaint Dutch tiles illustrating the Scriptures—yet Marley's face comes like the ancient Prophet's rod and swallows up the whole.

SCROOGE
Humbug!

He walks across the room, sits again. His glance rests upon a disused bell. With great astonishment and strange inexplicable dread he sees this bell begin to swing. Softly at first; then it rings out loudly, and so does every bell in the house.

(SOUND: soft swing building to every bell in the house pealing)

The bells cease together. Succeeded by a clanking noise deep down below, as if some person were dragging a heavy chain over the casks in the wine-merchant's cellar. The cellar-door flies open with a booming sound. The noise comes up the stairs, straight towards his door.

SCROOGE
It's humbug still! I won't believe it.

Without a pause it comes on through the heavy door and passes into the room. The dying flame leaps up as though it cried "I know him; Marley's Ghost!" and falls again.

JACOB MARLEY stands before him: same face, pigtail, usual waistcoat, tights and boots; tassels bristling; chain clasped about his middle, long, wound about him like a tail, made of cash-boxes, keys, padlocks, ledgers, deeds, and heavy purses wrought in steel. Body transparent—Scrooge can see the two buttons on his coat behind. Folded kerchief bound about head and chin.

(SOUND: chain clank, flame leap and fall, death-cold silence)

SCROOGE
How now! What do you want with me?

MARLEY
Much!

SCROOGE
Who are you?

MARLEY
Ask me who I was.

SCROOGE
Who were you then? You're particular, for a shade.

MARLEY
In life I was your partner, Jacob Marley.

SCROOGE
Can you—can you sit down?

MARLEY
I can.

SCROOGE
Do it, then.

The Ghost sits on the opposite side of the fireplace.

MARLEY
You don't believe in me.

SCROOGE
I don't.

MARLEY
What evidence would you have of my reality beyond that of your senses?

SCROOGE
I don't know.

MARLEY
Why do you doubt your senses?

SCROOGE
Because a little thing affects them. A slight disorder of the stomach makes them cheats.

SCROOGE
You may be an undigested bit of beef, a blot of mustard, a crumb of cheese, a fragment of an underdone potato.

SCROOGE
There's more of gravy than of grave about you, whatever you are!

The spirit raises a frightful cry and shakes its chain with dismal appalling noise. Scrooge holds tight to his chair. The phantom takes off the bandage round its head; its lower jaw drops down upon its breast.

(SOUND: frightful cry, chain shake, jaw drop)

Scrooge falls upon his knees, clasps his hands before his face.

SCROOGE
Mercy! Dreadful apparition, why do you trouble me?

MARLEY
Man of the worldly mind! Do you believe in me or not?

SCROOGE
I do. I must. But why do spirits walk the earth, and why do they come to me?

MARLEY
It is required of every man that the spirit within him should walk abroad among his fellowmen, and travel far and wide.

MARLEY
And if that spirit goes not forth in life, it is condemned to do so after death.

MARLEY
It is doomed to wander through the world—oh, woe is me!—and witness what it cannot share, but might have shared on earth, and turned to happiness!

Again the spectre raises a cry, shakes its chain, wrings its shadowy hands.

SCROOGE
You are fettered. Tell me why?

MARLEY
I wear the chain I forged in life. I made it link by link, and yard by yard; I girded it on of my own free will, and of my own free will I wore it.

MARLEY
Is its pattern strange to you?

Scrooge trembles more and more.

MARLEY
Or would you know the weight and length of the strong coil you bear yourself?

MARLEY
It was full as heavy and as long as this, seven Christmas Eves ago. You have laboured on it, since. It is a ponderous chain!

Scrooge glances about the floor expecting iron cable—sees nothing.

SCROOGE
Jacob. Old Jacob Marley, tell me more. Speak comfort to me, Jacob!

MARLEY
I have none to give. It comes from other regions, Ebenezer Scrooge, and is conveyed by other ministers, to other kinds of men.

MARLEY
I cannot rest, I cannot stay, I cannot linger anywhere. My spirit never walked beyond our counting-house—mark me!—in life my spirit never roved beyond the narrow limits of our money-changing hole; and weary journeys lie before me!

SCROOGE
You must have been very slow about it, Jacob.

MARLEY
Slow!

SCROOGE
Seven years dead. And travelling all the time!

MARLEY
The whole time. No rest, no peace. Incessant torture of remorse.

SCROOGE
You travel fast?

MARLEY
On the wings of the wind.

SCROOGE
You might have got over a great quantity of ground in seven years.

The Ghost sets up another cry and clanks its chain hideously.

MARLEY
Oh! captive, bound, and double-ironed, not to know that any Christian spirit working kindly in its little sphere will find its mortal life too short for its vast means of usefulness.

MARLEY
Not to know that no space of regret can make amends for one life's opportunity misused! Yet such was I! Oh! such was I!

SCROOGE
But you were always a good man of business, Jacob.

MARLEY
Business! Mankind was my business. The common welfare was my business; charity, mercy, forbearance, and benevolence, were, all, my business.

MARLEY
The dealings of my trade were but a drop of water in the comprehensive ocean of my business!

It holds up its chain at arm's length and flings it heavily upon the ground.

MARLEY
At this time of the rolling year I suffer most. Why did I walk through crowds of fellow-beings with my eyes turned down, and never raise them to that blessed Star which led the Wise Men to a poor abode!

SCROOGE
(dismayed, quaking)
Hear me!

MARLEY
My time is nearly gone.

SCROOGE
I will. But don't be hard upon me! Don't be flowery, Jacob! Pray!

MARLEY
I am here to-night to warn you, that you have yet a chance and hope of escaping my fate. A chance and hope of my procuring, Ebenezer.

SCROOGE
You were always a good friend to me. Thank'ee!

MARLEY
You will be haunted by Three Spirits.

Scrooge's countenance falls almost as low as the Ghost's.

SCROOGE
Is that the chance and hope you mentioned, Jacob?

MARLEY
It is.

SCROOGE
I—I think I'd rather not.

MARLEY
Without their visits, you cannot hope to shun the path I tread. Expect the first to-morrow, when the bell tolls One.

SCROOGE
Couldn't I take 'em all at once, and have it over, Jacob?

MARLEY
Expect the second on the next night at the same hour. The third upon the next night when the last stroke of Twelve has ceased to vibrate.

MARLEY
Look to see me no more; and look that, for your own sake, you remember what has passed between us!

The spectre binds the wrapper round its head again. It walks backward; at every step the window raises itself a little, until wide open. It beckons Scrooge. When within two paces, Marley's Ghost holds up its hand. Confused noises in the air: lamentation, regret, wailings inexpressibly sorrowful and self-accusatory. The spectre joins the mournful dirge and floats out upon the bleak dark night.

(SOUND: window rising, air filled with phantom wails, chain fading)

Scrooge follows to the window. The air is filled with phantoms, wandering hither and thither in restless haste, moaning; every one wears chains like Marley's; some linked together; none free. One old ghost in a white waistcoat with a monstrous iron safe attached to its ankle cries piteously at being unable to assist a wretched woman with an infant on a door-step. The misery with them all is that they sought to interfere for good in human matters and had lost the power for ever.

They fade into mist. Scrooge closes the window, examines the door—double-locked as he locked it, bolts undisturbed. He tries to say "Humbug!" but stops at the first syllable. Much in need of repose, he goes straight to bed without undressing and falls asleep upon the instant.

(SOUND: phantom moans fading, window shut, silence)

INT. SCROOGE'S GLOOMY CHAMBERS - NIGHT

Scrooge awakes. So dark he can scarcely distinguish the transparent window from the opaque walls. Neighbouring church chimes strike the four quarters. The heavy bell goes on from six to seven, seven to eight, regularly up to twelve—then stops. Twelve! It was past two when he went to bed.

(SOUND: church chimes, heavy bell to twelve)

He touches the spring of his repeater: its rapid little pulse beats twelve and stops.

SCROOGE
Why, it isn't possible that I can have slept through a whole day and far into another night.

He gropes to the window, rubs frost off with his sleeve. Still very foggy and extremely cold; no noise of people. He goes to bed again, thinks and thinks. Marley's Ghost bothers him exceedingly. Was it a dream or not?

He lies until the chime has gone three quarters more. He remembers the Ghost warned him of a visitation when the bell tolled One. He resolves to lie awake.

(SOUND: quarter chimes — ding dong)

SCROOGE
A quarter past.

(SOUND: ding dong)

SCROOGE
Half-past!

(SOUND: ding dong)

SCROOGE
A quarter to it.

(SOUND: ding dong)

SCROOGE
The hour itself, and nothing else!

He speaks before the hour bell sounds—which it now does with a deep, dull, hollow, melancholy ONE. Light flashes up in the room upon the instant. The curtains of his bed are drawn aside by a hand—those to which his face is addressed.

(SOUND: deep melancholy ONE, sudden light)

SCROOGE, starting up half-recumbent, finds himself face to face with the unearthly visitor: strange figure like a child yet like an old man diminished to a child's proportions; hair white as if with age yet face without a wrinkle, tenderest bloom on the skin; arms long and muscular; legs and feet delicately formed, bare; tunic of the purest white; lustrous belt; branch of fresh green holly in hand; dress trimmed with summer flowers; from the crown of its head a bright clear jet of light; great extinguisher for a cap held under its arm. The figure fluctuates in distinctness—now one arm, now one leg, now twenty legs, now a head without a body—then distinct and clear as ever.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
(soft, gentle, singularly low)
Are you the Spirit, sir, whose coming was foretold to me?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am!

SCROOGE
Who, and what are you?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am the Ghost of Christmas Past.

SCROOGE
Long Past?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
No. Your past.

Scrooge has a special desire to see the Spirit in his cap and begs him to be covered.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
What! Would you so soon put out, with worldly hands, the light I give?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Is it not enough that you are one of those whose passions made this cap, and force me through whole trains of years to wear it low upon my brow!

Scrooge reverently disclaims all intention to offend. He inquires what business brought him there.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Your welfare!

Scrooge thinks a night of unbroken rest would have been more conducive. The Spirit hears him thinking.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Your reclamation, then. Take heed!

It puts out its strong hand and clasps him gently by the arm.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Rise! and walk with me!

Scrooge finds the Spirit makes towards the window; clasps his robe in supplication.

SCROOGE
I am a mortal, and liable to fall.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Bear but a touch of my hand there, and you shall be upheld in more than this!

The Spirit lays its hand upon his heart. As the words are spoken, they pass through the wall.

(SOUND: soft rush of air, city vanishing)

EXT. COUNTRY ROAD AND MARKET-TOWN OF SCROOGE'S BOYHOOD - DAY

Open country road, fields on either hand. The city has entirely vanished. Clear, cold winter day, snow upon the ground. Scrooge clasps his hands.

SCROOGE
Good Heaven! I was bred in this place. I was a boy here!

He is conscious of a thousand odours floating in the air, each connected with a thousand thoughts, hopes, joys, and cares long, long forgotten. His lip trembles.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Your lip is trembling. And what is that upon your cheek?

SCROOGE
(muttering)
A pimple.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
You recollect the way?

SCROOGE
Remember it! I could walk it blindfold.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Strange to have forgotten it for so many years! Let us go on.

They walk. Scrooge recognises every gate, post, and tree. A little market-town appears in the distance with its bridge, church, and winding river. Shaggy ponies trot towards them with boys upon their backs; country gigs and carts. All the boys in great spirits, shouting Merry Christmas as they part at cross-roads.

(SOUND: crisp air, merry shouts, pony hooves on frozen road)

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
These are but shadows of the things that have been. They have no consciousness of us.

Why is Scrooge rejoiced beyond all bounds to see them? Why does his cold eye glisten and his heart leap up?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
The school is not quite deserted. A solitary child, neglected by his friends, is left there still.

Scrooge says he knows it. And he sobs.

INT. DREARY BOARDING SCHOOL - DAY

Mansion of dull red brick, weathercock-surmounted cupola, bell hanging in it. Broken fortunes: walls damp and mossy, windows broken, gates decayed. Fowls cluck in the stables; coach-houses over-run with grass. Dreary hall, poorly furnished, cold, vast. Earthy savour, chilly bareness.

They cross the hall to a door at the back. It opens upon a long, bare, melancholy room, lines of plain deal forms and desks. At one of these a lonely boy—YOUNG SCROOGE—is reading near a feeble fire. Scrooge sits down upon a form and weeps to see his poor forgotten self as he used to be.

(SOUND: mice behind panelling, drip from half-thawed water-spout, idle swing of empty store-house door)

Not a latent echo but falls upon Scrooge's heart with a softening influence and gives freer passage to his tears. The Spirit touches him on the arm and points to his younger self.

Suddenly a man in foreign garments stands outside the window with an axe stuck in his belt, leading an ass laden with wood.

SCROOGE
(in ecstasy)
Why, it's Ali Baba! It's dear old honest Ali Baba! Yes, yes, I know!

SCROOGE
One Christmas time, when yonder solitary child was left here all alone, he did come, for the first time, just like that. Poor boy!

SCROOGE
And Valentine, and his wild brother, Orson; there they go! And the Sultan's Groom turned upside down by the Genii; there he is upon his head! Serve him right.

To hear Scrooge expending all the earnestness of his nature on such subjects, between laughing and crying, would have been a surprise to his business friends. Then, with rapid transition:

SCROOGE
Poor boy!

He cries again.

SCROOGE
I wish... but it's too late now.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
What is the matter?

SCROOGE
Nothing. There was a boy singing a Christmas Carol at my door last night. I should like to have given him something: that's all.

The Ghost smiles thoughtfully and waves its hand.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Let us see another Christmas!

Young Scrooge grows larger; the room becomes a little darker and more dirty. He is not reading now, but walking up and down despairingly. Scrooge looks anxiously towards the door.

It opens. FAN, a little girl much younger than the boy, comes darting in, puts her arms about his neck, often kissing him.

FAN
Dear, dear brother! I have come to bring you home, dear brother! To bring you home, home, home!

YOUNG SCROOGE
Home, little Fan?

FAN
Yes! Home, for good and all. Home, for ever and ever. Father is so much kinder than he used to be, that home's like Heaven!

FAN
He spoke so gently to me one dear night when I was going to bed, that I was not afraid to ask him once more if you might come home; and he said Yes, you should; and sent me in a coach to bring you.

FAN
And you're to be a man! And are never to come back here; but first, we're to be together all the Christmas long, and have the merriest time in all the world.

YOUNG SCROOGE
You are quite a woman, little Fan!

She claps her hands, laughs, stands on tiptoe to embrace him, drags him towards the door. A terrible voice in the hall cries for Master Scrooge's box. The schoolmaster appears, glares with ferocious condescension, shakes hands. In the shivering best-parlour he produces curiously light wine and curiously heavy cake. Master Scrooge's trunk is tied on the chaise; the children drive gaily down the garden-sweep.

(SOUND: coach wheels, hoar-frost dashed from evergreens, children's laughter)

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Always a delicate creature, whom a breath might have withered. But she had a large heart!

SCROOGE
So she had. You're right. I will not gainsay it, Spirit. God forbid!

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
She died a woman, and had, as I think, children.

SCROOGE
One child.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
True. Your nephew!

Scrooge seems uneasy.

SCROOGE
Yes.

Although they have but that moment left the school, they are now in the busy thoroughfares of a city. Christmas time again; evening; streets lighted up.

INT. FEZZIWIG'S WAREHOUSE - NIGHT

The Ghost stops at a certain warehouse door.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Do you know it?

SCROOGE
Know it! Was I apprenticed here!

They go in. An old gentleman in a Welsh wig sits behind such a high desk that if he had been two inches taller he must have knocked his head against the ceiling.

SCROOGE
Why, it's old Fezziwig! Bless his heart; it's Fezziwig alive again!

Old FEZZIWIG lays down his pen, looks up at the clock—seven. He rubs his hands, adjusts his capacious waistcoat, laughs all over himself from his shoes to his organ of benevolence, calls out in a comfortable, oily, rich, fat, jovial voice:

FEZZIWIG
Yo ho, there! Ebenezer! Dick!

Scrooge's former self, now grown a young man (SCROOGE APPRENTICE), comes briskly in with his fellow-'prentice DICK WILKINS.

SCROOGE
Dick Wilkins, to be sure! Bless me, yes. There he is. He was very much attached to me, was Dick. Poor Dick! Dear, dear!

FEZZIWIG
Yo ho, my boys! No more work to-night. Christmas Eve, Dick. Christmas, Ebenezer! Let's have the shutters up before a man can say Jack Robinson!

The two fellows charge into the street with the shutters—one, two, three—have them up—four, five, six—barred and pinned—seven, eight, nine—and come back before you could have got to twelve, panting like race-horses.

FEZZIWIG
Hilli-ho! Clear away, my lads, and let's have lots of room here! Hilli-ho, Dick! Chirrup, Ebenezer!

Clear away! Every movable packed off; floor swept and watered; lamps trimmed; fuel heaped upon the fire. The warehouse becomes as snug, warm, dry, and bright a ball-room as you would desire upon a winter's night.

(SOUND: shutters slamming, floor sweeping, fire roaring up, fiddle tuning like fifty stomach-aches)

In comes a fiddler with a music-book, up to the lofty desk. In comes Mrs. Fezziwig, one vast substantial smile. In come the three Miss Fezziwigs, beaming and lovable. In come the six young followers whose hearts they broke. In come all the young men and women employed in the business. In come the housemaid with her cousin the baker, the cook with her brother's particular friend the milkman, the boy from over the way, the girl from next door but one. Away they all go, twenty couple at once.

Old Fezziwig claps his hands to stop the dance.

FEZZIWIG
Well done!

There are more dances, forfeits, cake, negus, Cold Roast, Cold Boiled, mince-pies, plenty of beer. The great effect comes when the fiddler strikes up "Sir Roger de Coverley." Old Fezziwig stands out to dance with Mrs. Fezziwig—top couple; three or four and twenty pair of partners. A positive light appears to issue from Fezziwig's calves; they shine like moons. He "cuts" so deftly he appears to wink with his legs, and comes upon his feet again without a stagger.

(SOUND: fiddle, laughter, dancing feet, porter pot)

When the clock strikes eleven, the domestic ball breaks up. Mr. and Mrs. Fezziwig take their stations on either side of the door and shake hands with every person individually, wishing a Merry Christmas. The lads are left to their beds under a counter in the back-shop.

During the whole time Scrooge has acted like a man out of his wits. His heart and soul are in the scene. It is not until now that he remembers the Ghost, looking full upon him, light upon its head burning very clear.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
A small matter to make these silly folks so full of gratitude.

SCROOGE
Small!

The Spirit signs him to listen to the two apprentices pouring out their hearts in praise of Fezziwig.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Why! Is it not? He has spent but a few pounds of your mortal money: three or four perhaps. Is that so much that he deserves this praise?

SCROOGE
(heated, speaking like his former self)
It isn't that, Spirit. He has the power to render us happy or unhappy; to make our service light or burdensome; a pleasure or a toil.

SCROOGE
Say that his power lies in words and looks; in things so slight and insignificant that it is impossible to add and count 'em up: what then? The happiness he gives is quite as great as if it cost a fortune.

He feels the Spirit's glance and stops.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
What is the matter?

SCROOGE
Nothing particular.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Something, I think?

SCROOGE
No. No. I should like to be able to say a word or two to my clerk just now. That's all.

His former self turns down the lamps. Scrooge and the Ghost again stand side by side in the open air.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
My time grows short. Quick!

Again Scrooge sees himself. He is older now; a man in the prime of life. His face has begun to wear the signs of care and avarice. There is an eager, greedy, restless motion in the eye. He sits by the side of BELLE, a fair young girl in a mourning-dress; tears in her eyes sparkle in the light from the Ghost.

INT. BELLE'S MODEST COMFORTABLE HOME - NIGHT

BELLE
It matters little. To you, very little. Another idol has displaced me; and if it can cheer and comfort you in time to come, as I would have tried to do, I have no just cause to grieve.

SCROOGE (PRIME)
What Idol has displaced you?

BELLE
A golden one.

SCROOGE (PRIME)
This is the even-handed dealing of the world! There is nothing on which it is so hard as poverty; and there is nothing it professes to condemn with such severity as the pursuit of wealth!

BELLE
You fear the world too much. All your other hopes have merged into the hope of being beyond the chance of its sordid reproach.

BELLE
I have seen your nobler aspirations fall off one by one, until the master-passion, Gain, engrosses you. Have I not?

SCROOGE (PRIME)
What then? Even if I have grown so much wiser, what then? I am not changed towards you.

She shakes her head.

SCROOGE (PRIME)
Am I?

BELLE
Our contract is an old one. It was made when we were both poor and content to be so, until, in good season, we could improve our worldly fortune by our patient industry.

BELLE
You are changed. When it was made, you were another man.

SCROOGE (PRIME)
I was a boy.

BELLE
Your own feeling tells you that you were not what you are. I am. That which promised happiness when we were one in heart is fraught with misery now that we are two.

BELLE
How often and how keenly I have thought of this, I will not say. It is enough that I have thought of it, and can release you.

SCROOGE (PRIME)
Have I ever sought release?

BELLE
In words. No. Never.

SCROOGE (PRIME)
In what, then?

BELLE
In a changed nature; in an altered spirit; in another atmosphere of life; another Hope as its great end.

BELLE
In everything that made my love of any worth or value in your sight. If this had never been between us, tell me, would you seek me out and try to win me now? Ah, no!

He seems to yield to the justice of this.

SCROOGE (PRIME)
You think not.

BELLE
I would gladly think otherwise if I could. Heaven knows! When I have learned a Truth like this, I know how strong and irresistible it must be.

BELLE
But if you were free to-day, to-morrow, yesterday, can even I believe that you would choose a dowerless girl—you who weigh everything by Gain?

BELLE
Or, choosing her, do I not know that your repentance and regret would surely follow? I do; and I release you. With a full heart, for the love of him you once were.

She leaves him, and they part.

SCROOGE
Spirit! Show me no more! Conduct me home. Why do you delight to torture me?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
One shadow more!

SCROOGE
No more! No more. I don't wish to see it. Show me no more!

But the relentless Ghost pinions him in both his arms and forces him to observe what happens next.

Another scene and place: a room not very large or handsome, but full of comfort. Near the winter fire sits a beautiful young girl so like the last that Scrooge believes it is the same—until he sees her now a comely matron, sitting opposite her daughter. More children than Scrooge can count; every child conducting itself like forty. Tumultuous, uproarious; mother and daughter laugh heartily.

A knocking at the door. Rush. The father comes home attended by a man laden with Christmas toys and presents. Shouting, struggling, onslaught on the defenceless porter. Development of every package. Joy, gratitude, ecstasy. By degrees the children subside upstairs.

Scrooge looks on more attentively than ever when the master of the house, daughter leaning fondly on him, sits down with her and her mother at his own fireside. When he thinks that such another creature might have called him father, and been a spring-time in the haggard winter of his life, his sight grows very dim indeed.

HUSBAND
Belle, I saw an old friend of yours this afternoon.

BELLE (MATRON)
Who was it?

HUSBAND
Guess!

BELLE (MATRON)
How can I? Tut, don't I know? Mr. Scrooge.

HUSBAND
Mr. Scrooge it was. I passed his office window; and as it was not shut up, and he had a candle inside, I could scarcely help seeing him.

HUSBAND
His partner lies upon the point of death, I hear; and there he sat alone. Quite alone in the world, I do believe.

SCROOGE
(broken voice)
Spirit! Remove me from this place.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I told you these were shadows of the things that have been. That they are what they are, do not blame me!

SCROOGE
Remove me! I cannot bear it!

He turns upon the Ghost. Seeing that it looks upon him with a face in which in some strange way there are fragments of all the faces it has shown him, he wrestles with it.

SCROOGE
Leave me! Take me back. Haunt me no longer!

In the struggle—if that can be called a struggle in which the Ghost with no visible resistance is undisturbed—Scrooge observes that its light is burning high and bright. Dimly connecting that with its influence over him, he seizes the extinguisher-cap and by a sudden action presses it down upon its head.

The Spirit drops beneath it, so that the extinguisher covers its whole form; but though Scrooge presses it down with all his force, he cannot hide the light, which streams from under it in an unbroken flood upon the ground.

(SOUND: struggle, light streaming, Scrooge's exhausted breath)

He is conscious of being exhausted, overcome by irresistible drowsiness, and of being in his own bedroom. He gives the cap a parting squeeze; his hand relaxes; he has barely time to reel to bed before he sinks into a heavy sleep.

INT. SCROOGE'S GLOOMY CHAMBERS - NIGHT

Awaking in the middle of a prodigiously tough snore, Scrooge sits up to get his thoughts together. He has no occasion to be told that the bell is again upon the stroke of One. He puts every curtain aside with his own hands and establishes a sharp look-out all round the bed.

(SOUND: tough snore cut short, bell approaching One)

Gentlemen of the free-and-easy sort express capacity for adventure from pitch-and-toss to manslaughter. Without venturing quite so hardily, Scrooge is ready for a good broad field of strange appearances—nothing between a baby and a rhinoceros would astonish him very much.

Being prepared for almost anything, he is not by any means prepared for nothing. When the Bell strikes One and no shape appears, he is taken with a violent fit of trembling. Five minutes, ten, a quarter of an hour. He lies upon his bed, the very core and centre of a blaze of ruddy light which streamed upon it when the clock proclaimed the hour—more alarming than a dozen ghosts, for he is powerless to make out what it means.

At last he begins to think the source of this ghostly light may be in the adjoining room. He gets up softly and shuffles in his slippers to the door.

(SOUND: clock One, silence, Scrooge's slippered shuffle)

The moment his hand is on the lock, a strange voice calls him by his name and bades him enter. He obeys.

It is his own room—but undergone a surprising transformation. Walls and ceiling hung with living green; bright gleaming berries; crisp leaves of holly, mistletoe, and ivy reflecting light like little mirrors. A mighty blaze roars up the chimney. Heaped up on the floor to form a kind of throne: turkeys, geese, game, poultry, brawn, great joints of meat, sucking-pigs, long wreaths of sausages, mince-pies, plum-puddings, barrels of oysters, red-hot chestnuts, cherry-cheeked apples, juicy oranges, luscious pears, immense twelfth-cakes, and seething bowls of punch that make the chamber dim with their delicious steam.

In easy state upon this couch sits a jolly Giant, glorious to see, who bears a glowing torch in shape not unlike Plenty's horn, and holds it up high to shed its light on Scrooge as he comes peeping round the door.

(SOUND: roaring fire, punch seething, torch crackle)

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Come in! Come in! and know me better, man!

Scrooge enters timidly and hangs his head. He is not the dogged Scrooge he had been.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I am the Ghost of Christmas Present. Look upon me!

Scrooge reverently does so. The Spirit is clothed in one simple green robe or mantle bordered with white fur, hanging so loosely that its capacious breast is bare. Feet bare. On its head no other covering than a holly wreath set here and there with shining icicles. Dark brown curls long and free; genial face, sparkling eye, open hand, cheery voice, unconstrained demeanour, joyful air. Girded round its middle an antique scabbard; no sword in it; the ancient sheath eaten up with rust.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
You have never seen the like of me before!

SCROOGE
Never.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Have never walked forth with the younger members of my family; meaning (for I am very young) my elder brothers born in these later years?

SCROOGE
I don't think I have. I am afraid I have not. Have you had many brothers, Spirit?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
More than eighteen hundred.

SCROOGE
A tremendous family to provide for!

The Ghost of Christmas Present rises.

SCROOGE
Spirit, conduct me where you will. I went forth last night on compulsion, and I learnt a lesson which is working now. To-night, if you have aught to teach me, let me profit by it.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Touch my robe!

Scrooge does as he is told and holds it fast. Holly, mistletoe, red berries, ivy, turkeys, geese, game, poultry, brawn, meat, pigs, sausages, oysters, pies, puddings, fruit, and punch all vanish instantly. So does the room, the fire, the ruddy glow, the hour of night. They stand in the city streets on Christmas morning.

(SOUND: sudden hush, then Christmas morning street bustle)

EXT. CITY STREETS ON CHRISTMAS MORNING - DAY

Severe weather. People make a rough but brisk and not unpleasant kind of music scraping snow from pavement and from tops of houses; boys in mad delight watch it come plumping down into the road, splitting into artificial little snow-storms. House fronts black, windows blacker; smooth white sheet of snow upon the roofs; dirtier snow upon the ground ploughed into deep furrows by heavy wheels. Gloomy sky; dingy mist half thawed, half frozen; sooty atoms as if all the chimneys in Great Britain had caught fire.

Yet there is an air of cheerfulness abroad that the clearest summer air and brightest summer sun might have endeavoured to diffuse in vain. People on housetops jovial and full of glee; exchanging facetious snowballs. Poulterers' shops still half open; fruiterers' radiant in their glory. Great round pot-bellied baskets of chestnuts; ruddy brown-faced Spanish Onions; pears and apples clustered high; bunches of grapes dangling; piles of filberts; Norfolk Biffins; gold and silver fish gasping round their little world.

(SOUND: snow scrapers, laughter, shop bells, church steeples calling)

The Grocers'! Nearly closed, perhaps two shutters down or one; but through those gaps such glimpses! Scales descending make a merry sound; twine and roller part company briskly; canisters rattled; blended scents of tea and coffee; raisins plentiful; almonds extremely white; sticks of cinnamon long and straight; candied fruits caked and spotted with molten sugar. Customers hurried and eager, tumbling up against each other, crashing wicker baskets, leaving purchases and running back—all in the best humour possible.

Steeples call good people all to church and chapel. Away they come flocking in their best clothes and gayest faces. From scores of bye-streets, lanes, and nameless turnings, innumerable people carrying their dinners to the bakers' shops.

The Spirit stands with Scrooge beside him in a baker's doorway, taking off the covers as their bearers pass, sprinkling incense on their dinners from his torch. Once or twice when there are angry words between some dinner-carriers who have jostled each other, he sheds a few drops of water on them from it, and their good humour is restored directly.

(SOUND: church bells, dinner covers lifted, torch hiss, restored laughter)

DINNER CARRIER
It was a shame to quarrel upon Christmas Day. And so it was! God love it, so it was!

SCROOGE
Is there a peculiar flavour in what you sprinkle from your torch?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
There is. My own.

SCROOGE
Would it apply to any kind of dinner on this day?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
To any kindly given. To a poor one most.

SCROOGE
Why to a poor one most?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Because it needs it most.

SCROOGE
Spirit, I wonder you, of all the beings in the many worlds about us, should desire to cramp these people's opportunities of innocent enjoyment.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I!

SCROOGE
You would deprive them of their means of dining every seventh day, often the only day on which they can be said to dine at all. Wouldn't you?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I!

SCROOGE
You seek to close these places on the Seventh Day? And it comes to the same thing.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I seek!

SCROOGE
Forgive me if I am wrong. It has been done in your name, or at least in that of your family.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
There are some upon this earth of yours who lay claim to know us, and who do their deeds of passion, pride, ill-will, hatred, envy, bigotry, and selfishness in our name.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Who are as strange to us and all our kith and kin as if they had never lived. Remember that, and charge their doings on themselves, not us.

Scrooge promises that he will. They go on, invisible, into the suburbs. The Ghost, notwithstanding his gigantic size, accommodates himself to any place with ease. Perhaps it is the pleasure he has in showing off this power, or his own kind generous hearty nature and sympathy with all poor men, that leads him straight to Scrooge's clerk's.

INT. BOB CRATCHIT'S FOUR-ROOMED HOUSE IN CAMDEN TOWN - DAY

On the threshold the Spirit smiles and stops to bless Bob Cratchit's dwelling with the sprinkling of his torch. Bob has but fifteen Bob a-week himself; and yet the Ghost of Christmas Present blesses his four-roomed house!

(SOUND: torch blessing, then domestic bustle)

Up rises MRS. CRATCHIT, dressed out but poorly in a twice-turned gown, but brave in ribbons which are cheap and make a goodly show for sixpence. She lays the cloth, assisted by BELINDA CRATCHIT, second of her daughters, also brave in ribbons. MASTER PETER CRATCHIT plunges a fork into the saucepan of potatoes, getting the corners of his monstrous shirt collar (Bob's private property, conferred upon his son and heir in honour of the day) into his mouth. Two smaller Cratchits, boy and girl, come tearing in, screaming that outside the baker's they have smelt the goose and known it for their own; they dance about the table and exalt Master Peter to the skies while he blows the fire until the slow potatoes bubbling up knock loudly at the saucepan-lid.

MRS. CRATCHIT
What has ever got your precious father then? And your brother, Tiny Tim! And Martha warn't as late last Christmas Day by half-an-hour?

GIRL CRATCHIT
Here's Martha, mother!

The two young Cratchits:
Hurrah! There's such a goose, Martha!

MRS. CRATCHIT
Why, bless your heart alive, my dear, how late you are!

She kisses her a dozen times and takes off her shawl and bonnet with officious zeal.

MARTHA
We'd a deal of work to finish up last night, and had to clear away this morning, mother!

MRS. CRATCHIT
Well! Never mind so long as you are come. Sit ye down before the fire, my dear, and have a warm, Lord bless ye!

TWO YOUNG CRATCHITS
No, no! There's father coming! Hide, Martha, hide!

Martha hides. In comes little Bob, the father, with at least three feet of comforter exclusive of the fringe hanging down before him; threadbare clothes darned up and brushed to look seasonable; and TINY TIM upon his shoulder. Alas for Tiny Tim, he bears a little crutch, and has his limbs supported by an iron frame!

BOB CRATCHIT
Why, where's our Martha?

MRS. CRATCHIT
Not coming.

BOB CRATCHIT
Not coming! Not coming upon Christmas Day!

Martha doesn't like to see him disappointed, even in joke; she comes out prematurely from behind the closet door and runs into his arms. The two young Cratchits hustle Tiny Tim off into the wash-house that he may hear the pudding singing in the copper.

MRS. CRATCHIT
And how did little Tim behave?

BOB CRATCHIT
As good as gold, and better. Somehow he gets thoughtful, sitting by himself so much, and thinks the strangest things you ever heard.

BOB CRATCHIT
He told me, coming home, that he hoped the people saw him in the church, because he was a cripple, and it might be pleasant to them to remember upon Christmas Day who made lame beggars walk, and blind men see.

Bob's voice is tremulous; trembles more when he says that Tiny Tim is growing strong and hearty.

Tiny Tim's active little crutch is heard upon the floor; back he comes escorted by brother and sister to his stool before the fire. Bob, turning up his cuffs, compounds some hot mixture in a jug with gin and lemons, stirs it round and round and puts it on the hob to simmer. Master Peter and the two ubiquitous young Cratchits fetch the goose; high procession.

(SOUND: crutch on floor, pudding singing in copper, goose procession)

Such a bustle ensues that you might have thought a goose the rarest of all birds. Mrs. Cratchit makes the gravy hissing hot; Master Peter mashes the potatoes with incredible vigour; Miss Belinda sweetens up the apple-sauce; Martha dusts the hot plates; Bob takes Tiny Tim beside him in a tiny corner at the table; the two young Cratchits set chairs for everybody and cram spoons into their mouths lest they should shriek for goose before their turn. At last the dishes are set on, and grace is said. Breathless pause as Mrs. Cratchit, looking slowly all along the carving-knife, prepares to plunge it in the breast. When she does, and the long expected gush of stuffing issues forth, one murmur of delight arises all round the board. Even Tiny Tim, excited by the two young Cratchits, beats on the table with the handle of his knife and feebly cries Hurrah!

There never was such a goose. Bob said he didn't believe there ever was such a goose cooked. Its tenderness and flavour, size and cheapness, themes of universal admiration. Eked out by apple-sauce and mashed potatoes, a sufficient dinner for the whole family. As Mrs. Cratchit said with great delight (surveying one small atom of a bone upon the dish), they hadn't ate it all at last! Yet every one had had enough.

Now the plates being changed by Miss Belinda, Mrs. Cratchit leaves the room alone—too nervous to bear witnesses—to take the pudding up and bring it in.

Suppose it should not be done enough! Suppose it should break in turning out! Suppose somebody should have got over the wall of the back-yard and stolen it while they were merry with the goose—a supposition at which the two young Cratchits become livid!

Hallo! A great deal of steam! The pudding is out of the copper. A smell like a washing-day! That was the cloth. A smell like an eating-house and a pastrycook's next door to each other, with a laundress's next door to that! That was the pudding! In half a minute Mrs. Cratchit enters—flushed, but smiling proudly—with the pudding, like a speckled cannon-ball, so hard and firm, blazing in half of half-a-quartern of ignited brandy, and bedight with Christmas holly stuck into the top.

(SOUND: pudding blaze whoosh, family gasp of wonder)

Oh, a wonderful pudding! Bob Cratchit said, and calmly too, that he regarded it as the greatest success achieved by Mrs. Cratchit since their marriage. Mrs. Cratchit said that now the weight was off her mind, she would confess she had had her doubts about the quantity of flour. Everybody had something to say about it, but nobody said or thought it was at all a small pudding for a large family. It would have been flat heresy to do so.

At last the dinner is all done, the cloth cleared, the hearth swept, the fire made up. The compound in the jug tasted and considered perfect; apples and oranges put upon the table; a shovel-full of chestnuts on the fire. All the Cratchit family draw round the hearth in what Bob Cratchit called a circle, meaning half a one. At Bob Cratchit's elbow stands the family display of glass: two tumblers, and a custard-cup without a handle. These hold the hot stuff from the jug as well as golden goblets would have done. Bob serves it out with beaming looks while the chestnuts on the fire sputter and crack noisily. Then Bob proposes:

BOB CRATCHIT
A Merry Christmas to us all, my dears. God bless us!

Which all the family re-echo.

TINY TIM
God bless us every one!

He sits very close to his father's side upon his little stool. Bob holds his withered little hand in his, as if he loved the child, and wished to keep him by his side, and dreaded that he might be taken from him.

SCROOGE
Spirit, tell me if Tiny Tim will live.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I see a vacant seat in the poor chimney-corner, and a crutch without an owner, carefully preserved. If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, the child will die.

SCROOGE
No, no. Oh, no, kind Spirit! say he will be spared.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, none other of my race will find him here. What then? If he be like to die, he had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

Scrooge hangs his head to hear his own words quoted by the Spirit, and is overcome with penitence and grief.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Man, if man you be in heart, not adamant, forbear that wicked cant until you have discovered What the surplus is, and Where it is.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Will you decide what men shall live, what men shall die? It may be that in the sight of Heaven you are more worthless and less fit to live than millions like this poor man's child.

Scrooge bends before the Ghost's rebuke, trembling, casts his eyes upon the ground. But he raises them speedily on hearing his own name.

BOB CRATCHIT
Mr. Scrooge! I'll give you Mr. Scrooge, the Founder of the Feast!

MRS. CRATCHIT
The Founder of the Feast indeed! I wish I had him here. I'd give him a piece of my mind to feast upon, and I hope he'd have a good appetite for it.

BOB CRATCHIT
My dear, the children! Christmas Day.

MRS. CRATCHIT
It should be Christmas Day, I am sure, on which one drinks the health of such an odious, stingy, hard, unfeeling man as Mr. Scrooge. You know he is, Robert! Nobody knows it better than you do, poor fellow!

BOB CRATCHIT
My dear, Christmas Day.

MRS. CRATCHIT
I'll drink his health for your sake and the Day's, not for his. Long life to him! A merry Christmas and a happy new year! He'll be very merry and very happy, I have no doubt!

The children drink the toast after her. It is the first of their proceedings which has no heartiness. Tiny Tim drinks it last of all, but he doesn't care twopence for it. Scrooge is the Ogre of the family. The mention of his name casts a dark shadow on the party, which is not dispelled for full five minutes.

After it has passed away, they are ten times merrier than before. Bob tells them how he has a situation in his eye for Master Peter which would bring in, if obtained, full five-and-sixpence weekly. Martha, poor apprentice at a milliner's, tells what kind of work she has to do. By-and-bye they have a song about a lost child travelling in the snow, from Tiny Tim, who has a plaintive little voice and sings it very well indeed.

There is nothing of high mark in this. They are not a handsome family; not well dressed; their shoes far from water-proof; their clothes scanty. But they are happy, grateful, pleased with one another, and contented with the time. When they fade, and look happier yet in the bright sprinklings of the Spirit's torch at parting, Scrooge has his eye upon them, and especially on Tiny Tim, until the last.

(SOUND: chestnuts cracking, Tiny Tim's plaintive song, torch sprinkle)

EXT. BLEAK MOOR AND MINERS' HUT - NIGHT

By this time it is getting dark and snowing pretty heavily. Without a word of warning from the Ghost, they stand upon a bleak and desert moor where monstrous masses of rude stone are cast about as though it were the burial-place of giants; water spread itself wheresoever it listed, or would have done so but for the frost that held it prisoner; nothing grew but moss and furze and coarse rank grass. Down in the west the setting sun has left a streak of fiery red which glares upon the desolation for an instant like a sullen eye, then is lost in the thick gloom of darkest night.

SCROOGE
What place is this?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
A place where Miners live, who labour in the bowels of the earth. But they know me. See!

A light shines from the window of a hut. Swiftly they advance. Passing through the wall of mud and stone, they find a cheerful company assembled round a glowing fire. An old, old man and woman, with their children and their children's children, and another generation beyond that, all decked out gaily in their holiday attire. The old man, in a voice that seldom rises above the howling of the wind upon the barren waste, is singing them a Christmas song—it had been a very old song when he was a boy—and from time to time they all join in the chorus.

(SOUND: howling wind, old man's song, chorus rising)

The Spirit does not tarry here, but bades Scrooge hold his robe, and passing on above the moor, speeds—whither? Not to sea? To sea.

EXT. SOLITARY LIGHTHOUSE ON REEF - NIGHT

Built upon a dismal reef of sunken rocks, some league or so from shore, on which the waters chafe and dash the wild year through, there stands a solitary lighthouse. Great heaps of sea-weed cling to its base; storm-birds rise and fall about it like the waves they skim.

But even here, two men who watch the light have made a fire that through the loophole in the thick stone wall sheds out a ray of brightness on the awful sea. Joining their horny hands over the rough table at which they sit, they wish each other Merry Christmas in their can of grog; and one of them, the elder, with his face all damaged and scarred with hard weather as the figure-head of an old ship might be, strikes up a sturdy song that is like a Gale in itself.

(SOUND: thundering water, gale-song, grog can)

EXT. SHIP AT SEA - NIGHT

Again the Ghost speeds on, above the black and heaving sea—on, on—until, being far away from any shore, they light on a ship. They stand beside the helmsman at the wheel, the look-out in the bow, the officers who have the watch; dark, ghostly figures in their several stations. But every man among them hums a Christmas tune, or has a Christmas thought, or speaks below his breath to his companion of some bygone Christmas Day, with homeward hopes belonging to it. And every man on board, waking or sleeping, good or bad, has had a kinder word for another on that day than on any day in the year; and has shared to some extent in its festivities; and has remembered those he cared for at a distance, and has known that they delighted to remember him.

(SOUND: wind moaning, lonely darkness over unknown abyss, hummed Christmas tune)

It is a great surprise to Scrooge, while listening to the moaning of the wind and thinking what a solemn thing it is to move on through the lonely darkness over an unknown abyss whose depths are secrets as profound as Death—it is a great surprise to Scrooge to hear a hearty laugh. It is a much greater surprise to recognise it as his own nephew's, and to find himself in a bright, dry, gleaming room, with the Spirit standing smiling by his side.

INT. FRED'S BRIGHT DINING-ROOM - NIGHT

FRED
Ha, ha! Ha, ha, ha!

If you should happen to know a man more blest in a laugh than Scrooge's nephew, all one can say is one should like to know him too. There is nothing in the world so irresistibly contagious as laughter and good-humour. When Scrooge's nephew laughs in this way—holding his sides, rolling his head, twisting his face into the most extravagant contortions—Scrooge's niece by marriage laughs as heartily as he. And their assembled friends, not a bit behindhand, roar out lustily.

FRED
He said that Christmas was a humbug, as I live! He believed it too!

SCROOGE'S NIECE
More shame for him, Fred!

She is very pretty: exceedingly pretty. Dimpled, surprised-looking, capital face; ripe little mouth that seemed made to be kissed; all kinds of good little dots about her chin that melted into one another when she laughed; sunniest pair of eyes you ever saw. Altogether provoking, you know; but satisfactory, too. Oh, perfectly satisfactory.

FRED
He's a comical old fellow, that's the truth: and not so pleasant as he might be. However, his offences carry their own punishment, and I have nothing to say against him.

SCROOGE'S NIECE
I'm sure he is very rich, Fred. At least you always tell me so.

FRED
What of that, my dear! His wealth is of no use to him. He don't do any good with it. He don't make himself comfortable with it.

FRED
He hasn't the satisfaction of thinking—ha, ha, ha!—that he is ever going to benefit US with it.

SCROOGE'S NIECE
I have no patience with him.

Scrooge's niece's sisters and all the other ladies express the same opinion.

FRED
Oh, I have! I am sorry for him; I couldn't be angry with him if I tried. Who suffers by his ill whims! Himself, always.

FRED
Here, he takes it into his head to dislike us, and he won't come and dine with us. What's the consequence? He don't lose much of a dinner.

SCROOGE'S NIECE
Indeed, I think he loses a very good dinner.

Everybody else says the same; they have just had dinner, and with the dessert upon the table are clustered round the fire by lamplight.

FRED
Well! I'm very glad to hear it, because I haven't great faith in these young housekeepers. What do you say, Topper?

TOPPER has clearly got his eye upon one of Scrooge's niece's sisters; he answers that a bachelor is a wretched outcast who has no right to express an opinion on the subject. Whereat Scrooge's niece's sister—the plump one with the lace tucker—blushes.

SCROOGE'S NIECE
Do go on, Fred. He never finishes what he begins to say! He is such a ridiculous fellow!

Fred revels in another laugh; the infection is unanimous.

FRED
I was only going to say that the consequence of his taking a dislike to us, and not making merry with us, is, as I think, that he loses some pleasant moments, which could do him no harm.

FRED
I am sure he loses pleasanter companions than he can find in his own thoughts, either in his mouldy old office, or his dusty chambers.

FRED
I mean to give him the same chance every year, whether he likes it or not, for I pity him. He may rail at Christmas till he dies, but he can't help thinking better of it—I defy him—if he finds me going there, in good temper, year after year, and saying Uncle Scrooge, how are you?

FRED
If it only puts him in the vein to leave his poor clerk fifty pounds, that's something; and I think I shook him yesterday.

It is their turn to laugh now at the notion of his shaking Scrooge. Being thoroughly good-natured, he encourages them and passes the bottle joyously.

After tea, they have some music. They are a musical family. Scrooge's niece plays well upon the harp; and plays among other tunes a simple little air which had been familiar to the child who fetched Scrooge from the boarding-school. When this strain of music sounds, all the things that Ghost of Christmas Past had shown him come upon his mind; he softens more and more; and thinks that if he could have listened to it often, years ago, he might have cultivated the kindnesses of life for his own happiness with his own hands, without resorting to the sexton's spade that buried Jacob Marley.

(SOUND: harp simple air, laughter, fire crackle)

But they don't devote the whole evening to music. After a while they play at forfeits; for it is good to be children sometimes, and never better than at Christmas, when its mighty Founder was a child himself. First a game at blind-man's buff. Of course there was. Topper is not really blind; it is a done thing between him and Scrooge's nephew; and the Ghost of Christmas Present knows it. The way he goes after that plump sister in the lace tucker is an outrage on the credulity of human nature. Knocking down the fire-irons, tumbling over the chairs, bumping against the piano, smothering himself among the curtains—wherever she went, there went he! He always knew where the plump sister was. He wouldn't catch anybody else. When at last he catches her, his conduct is the most execrable: pretending not to know her; pretending it is necessary to touch her head-dress, and further to assure himself of her identity by pressing a certain ring upon her finger, and a certain chain about her neck.

Scrooge's niece is not one of the blind-man's buff party, but is made comfortable with a large chair and a footstool in a snug corner, where the Ghost and Scrooge are close behind her. But she joins in the forfeits, and loves her love to admiration with all the letters of the alphabet. Likewise at the game of How, When, and Where, she is very great.

There might have been twenty people there, young and old, but they all played, and so did Scrooge; for wholly forgetting in the interest he had in what was going on that his voice made no sound in their ears, he sometimes came out with his guess quite loud, and very often guessed quite right, too.

The Ghost is greatly pleased to find him in this mood, and looks upon him with such favour that he begs like a boy to be allowed to stay until the guests depart. But this the Spirit says cannot be done.

SCROOGE
Here is a new game. One half hour, Spirit, only one!

It is a Game called Yes and No, where Scrooge's nephew has to think of something, and the rest must find out what; he only answering to their questions yes or no. The brisk fire of questioning elicits that he is thinking of an animal, a live animal, rather a disagreeable animal, a savage animal, an animal that growled and grunted sometimes, and talked sometimes, and lived in London, and walked about the streets, and wasn't made a show of, and wasn't led by anybody, and didn't live in a menagerie, and was never killed in a market, and was not a horse, or an ass, or a cow, or a bull, or a tiger, or a dog, or a pig, or a cat, or a bear. At every fresh question this nephew bursts into a fresh roar of laughter. At last the plump sister, falling into a similar state, cries out:

PLUMP SISTER
I have found it out! I know what it is, Fred! I know what it is!

FRED
What is it?

PLUMP SISTER
It's your Uncle Scro-o-o-o-oge!

Which it certainly was. Admiration is the universal sentiment, though some object that the reply to "Is it a bear?" ought to have been "Yes."

FRED
He has given us plenty of merriment, I am sure, and it would be ungrateful not to drink his health. Here is a glass of mulled wine ready to our hand at the moment; and I say, 'Uncle Scrooge!'

ALL
Well! Uncle Scrooge!

FRED
A Merry Christmas and a Happy New Year to the old man, whatever he is! He wouldn't take it from me, but may he have it, nevertheless. Uncle Scrooge!

Uncle Scrooge has imperceptibly become so gay and light of heart that he would have pledged the unconscious company in return, and thanked them in an inaudible speech, if the Ghost had given him time. But the whole scene passes off in the breath of the last word spoken by his nephew; and he and the Spirit are again upon their travels.

(SOUND: mulled wine toast, laughter fading into travel wind)

Much they see, and far they go, and many homes they visit, but always with a happy end. The Spirit stands beside sick beds, and they are cheerful; on foreign lands, and they are close at home; by struggling men, and they are patient in their greater hope; by poverty, and it is rich. In almshouse, hospital, and jail, in misery's every refuge, where vain man in his little brief authority had not made fast the door and barred the Spirit out, he left his blessing, and taught Scrooge his precepts.

It is a long night, if it were only a night; but Scrooge has his doubts of this, because the Christmas Holidays appear to be condensed into the space of time they passed together. It is strange, too, that while Scrooge remains unaltered in his outward form, the Ghost grows older, clearly older. Scrooge has observed this change, but never spoke of it, until they leave a children's Twelfth Night party, when, looking at the Spirit as they stand together in an open place, he notices that its hair is grey.

SCROOGE
Are spirits' lives so short?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
My life upon this globe is very brief. It ends to-night.

SCROOGE
To-night!

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
To-night at midnight. Hark! The time is drawing near.

The chimes are ringing the three quarters past eleven at that moment.

(SOUND: chimes three quarters past eleven)

SCROOGE
Forgive me if I am not justified in what I ask, but I see something strange, and not belonging to yourself, protruding from your skirts. Is it a foot or a claw?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
It might be a claw, for the flesh there is upon it. Look here.

From the foldings of its robe, it brings two children; wretched, abject, frightful, hideous, miserable. They kneel down at its feet, and cling upon the outside of its garment.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Oh, Man! look here. Look, look, down here!

They are a boy and girl. Yellow, meagre, ragged, scowling, wolfish; but prostrate, too, in their humility. Where graceful youth should have filled their features out and touched them with its freshest tints, a stale and shrivelled hand, like that of age, has pinched and twisted them and pulled them into shreds. Where angels might have sat enthroned, devils lurked and glared out menacing. No change, no degradation, no perversion of humanity, in any grade, through all the mysteries of wonderful creation, has monsters half so horrible and dread.

Scrooge starts back, appalled. Having them shown to him in this way, he tries to say they are fine children, but the words choke themselves rather than be parties to a lie of such enormous magnitude.

SCROOGE
Spirit! are they yours?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
They are Man's. And they cling to me, appealing from their fathers. This boy is Ignorance. This girl is Want.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Beware them both, and all of their degree, but most of all beware this boy, for on his brow I see that written which is Doom, unless the writing be erased.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Deny it! Slander those who tell it ye! Admit it for your factious purposes, and make it worse. And bide the end!

SCROOGE
Have they no refuge or resource?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Are there no prisons? Are there no workhouses?

The bell strikes twelve.

(SOUND: bell striking twelve, Spirit vanishing)

Scrooge looks about him for the Ghost, and sees it not. As the last stroke ceases to vibrate, he remembers the prediction of old Jacob Marley, and lifting up his eyes, beholds a solemn Phantom, draped and hooded, coming like a mist along the ground towards him.

EXT. OPEN PLACE / INT. SCROOGE'S GLOOMY CHAMBERS - NIGHT

The Phantom slowly, gravely, silently approaches. When it comes near him, Scrooge bends down upon his knee; for in the very air through which this Spirit moves it seems to scatter gloom and mystery.

It is shrouded in a deep black garment which conceals its head, its face, its form, and leaves nothing of it visible save one outstretched hand. But for this it would have been difficult to detach its figure from the night, and separate it from the darkness by which it is surrounded.

He feels that it is tall and stately when it comes beside him, and that its mysterious presence fills him with a solemn dread. He knows no more, for the Spirit neither speaks nor moves.

(SOUND: silence so deep it presses; distant final vibration of twelve)

SCROOGE
I am in the presence of the Ghost of Christmas Yet To Come?

The Spirit answers not, but points onward with its hand.

SCROOGE
You are about to show me shadows of the things that have not happened, but will happen in the time before us. Is that so, Spirit?

The upper portion of the garment is contracted for an instant in its folds, as if the Spirit had inclined its head. That is the only answer he receives.

Although well used to ghostly company by this time, Scrooge fears the silent shape so much that his legs tremble beneath him, and he finds that he can hardly stand when he prepares to follow it. The Spirit pauses a moment, observing his condition, giving him time to recover.

But Scrooge is all the worse for this. It thrills him with a vague uncertain horror to know that behind the dusky shroud there are ghostly eyes intently fixed upon him, while he, though he stretches his own to the utmost, can see nothing but a spectral hand and one great heap of black.

SCROOGE
Ghost of the Future! I fear you more than any spectre I have seen. But as I know your purpose is to do me good, and as I hope to live to be another man from what I was, I am prepared to bear you company, and do it with a thankful heart. Will you not speak to me?

It gives him no reply. The hand is pointed straight before them.

SCROOGE
Lead on! Lead on! The night is waning fast, and it is precious time to me, I know. Lead on, Spirit!

The Phantom moves away as it had come towards him. Scrooge follows in the shadow of its dress, which bears him up, he thinks, and carries him along.

EXT. CITY / THE EXCHANGE - DAY

They scarcely seem to enter the city; for the city rather seems to spring up about them and encompass them of its own act. But there they are, in the heart of it; on 'Change, amongst the merchants; who hurry up and down, and chink the money in their pockets, and converse in groups, and look at their watches, and trifle thoughtfully with their great gold seals; and so forth, as Scrooge has seen them often.

The Spirit stops beside one little knot of business men. Observing that the hand is pointed to them, Scrooge advances to listen to their talk.

(SOUND: chink of money, watches, city bustle)

FAT MAN
No, I don't know much about it, either way. I only know he's dead.

BUSINESSMAN 2
When did he die?

FAT MAN
Last night, I believe.

BUSINESSMAN 3
Why, what was the matter with him? I thought he'd never die.

FAT MAN
(with a yawn)
God knows.

RED-FACED GENTLEMAN
What has he done with his money?

FAT MAN
I haven't heard. Left it to his Company, perhaps. He hasn't left it to me. That's all I know.

This pleasantry is received with a general laugh.

FAT MAN
It's likely to be a very cheap funeral, for upon my life I don't know of anybody to go to it. Suppose we make up a party and volunteer?

RED-FACED GENTLEMAN
I don't mind going if a lunch is provided. But I must be fed, if I make one.

Another laugh.

FAT MAN
Well, I am the most disinterested among you, after all, for I never wear black gloves, and I never eat lunch. But I'll offer to go, if anybody else will.

FAT MAN
When I come to think of it, I'm not at all sure that I wasn't his most particular friend; for we used to stop and speak whenever we met. Bye, bye!

Speakers and listeners stroll away and mix with other groups. Scrooge knows the men, and looks towards the Spirit for an explanation.

The Phantom glides on into a street. Its finger points to two persons meeting. Scrooge listens again.

He knows these men also, perfectly. They are men of business: very wealthy, and of great importance. He had made a point always of standing well in their esteem—in a business point of view, that is.

BUSINESSMAN A
How are you?

BUSINESSMAN B
How are you?

BUSINESSMAN A
Well! Old Scratch has got his own at last, hey?

BUSINESSMAN B
So I am told. Cold, isn't it?

BUSINESSMAN A
Seasonable for Christmas time. You're not a skater, I suppose?

BUSINESSMAN B
No. No. Something else to think of. Good morning!

Not another word. That was their meeting, their conversation, and their parting.

Scrooge is at first inclined to be surprised that the Spirit should attach importance to conversations apparently so trivial; but feeling assured that they must have some hidden purpose, he sets himself to consider what it is likely to be. They could scarcely be supposed to have any bearing on the death of Jacob, his old partner, for that was Past, and this Ghost's province was the Future. Nor could he think of any one immediately connected with himself to whom he could apply them. But nothing doubting that to whomsoever they applied they had some latent moral for his own improvement, he resolves to treasure up every word he hears and everything he sees; and especially to observe the shadow of himself when it appears. For he has an expectation that the conduct of his future self will give him the clue he missed, and will render the solution of these riddles easy.

He looks about in that very place for his own image; but another man stands in his accustomed corner, and though the clock points to his usual time of day for being there, he sees no likeness of himself among the multitudes that pour in through the Porch. It gives him little surprise, however; for he has been revolving in his mind a change of life, and thought and hoped he saw his new-born resolutions carried out in this.

Quiet and dark, beside him stands the Phantom, with its outstretched hand. When he rouses himself from his thoughtful quest, he fancies from the turn of the hand, and its situation in reference to himself, that the Unseen Eyes are looking at him keenly. It makes him shudder, and feel very cold.

They leave the busy scene and go into an obscure part of the town where Scrooge has never penetrated before, although he recognises its situation and its bad repute. The ways are foul and narrow; the shops and houses wretched; the people half-naked, drunken, slipshod, ugly. Alleys and archways, like so many cesspools, disgorge their offences of smell and dirt and life upon the straggling streets; and the whole quarter reeks with crime, with filth, and misery.

INT. OBSCURE FOUL QUARTER OF TOWN / OLD JOE'S SHOP - NIGHT

Far in this den of infamous resort, there is a low-browed, beetling shop, below a pent-house roof, where iron, old rags, bottles, bones, and greasy offal are bought. Upon the floor within are piled up heaps of rusty keys, nails, chains, hinges, files, scales, weights, and refuse iron of all kinds. Secrets that few would like to scrutinise are bred and hidden in mountains of unseemly rags, masses of corrupted fat, and sepulchres of bones. Sitting in among the wares he deals in, by a charcoal stove made of old bricks, is OLD JOE, a grey-haired rascal nearly seventy years of age, who has screened himself from the cold air without by a frousy curtaining of miscellaneous tatters hung upon a line, and smokes his pipe in all the luxury of calm retirement.

(SOUND: skreeking door hinges, charcoal stove, pipe draw)

Scrooge and the Phantom come into the presence of this man just as a woman with a heavy bundle slinks into the shop. But she has scarcely entered when another woman, similarly laden, comes in too; and she is closely followed by a man in faded black, who is no less startled by the sight of them than they have been upon the recognition of each other. After a short period of blank astonishment, in which the old man with the pipe has joined them, they all three burst into a laugh.

CHARWOMAN
Let the charwoman alone to be the first! Let the laundress alone to be the second; and let the undertaker's man alone to be the third. Look here, old Joe, here's a chance! If we haven't all three met here without meaning it!

OLD JOE
You couldn't have met in a better place. Come into the parlour. You were made free of it long ago, you know; and the other two an't strangers. Stop till I shut the door of the shop. Ah! How it skreeks! There an't such a rusty bit of metal in the place as its own hinges, I believe; and I'm sure there's no such old bones here as mine. Ha, ha! We're all suitable to our calling, we're well matched. Come into the parlour.

The parlour is the space behind the screen of rags. The old man rakes the fire together with an old stair-rod, and having trimmed his smoky lamp (for it is night) with the stem of his pipe, puts it in his mouth again.

While he does this, the woman who has already spoken throws her bundle on the floor and sits down in a flaunting manner on a stool; crossing her elbows on her knees, and looking with a bold defiance at the other two.

CHARWOMAN
What odds then! What odds, Mrs. Dilber? Every person has a right to take care of themselves. He always did.

MRS. DILBER
That's true, indeed! No man more so.

CHARWOMAN
Why then, don't stand staring as if you was afraid, woman; who's the wiser? We're not going to pick holes in each other's coats, I suppose?

MRS. DILBER
No, indeed! We should hope not.

CHARWOMAN
Very well, then! That's enough. Who's the worse for the loss of a few things like these? Not a dead man, I suppose.

MRS. DILBER
No, indeed.

CHARWOMAN
If he wanted to keep 'em after he was dead, a wicked old screw, why wasn't he natural in his lifetime? If he had been, he'd have had somebody to look after him when he was struck with Death, instead of lying gasping out his last there, alone by himself.

MRS. DILBER
It's the truest word that ever was spoke. It's a judgment on him.

CHARWOMAN
I wish it was a little heavier judgment, and it should have been, you may depend upon it, if I could have laid my hands on anything else. Open that bundle, old Joe, and let me know the value of it. Speak out plain. I'm not afraid to be the first, nor afraid for them to see it.

CHARWOMAN
We know pretty well that we were helping ourselves, before we met here, I believe. It's no sin. Open the bundle, Joe.

But the gallantry of her friends will not allow of this; and the man in faded black, mounting the breach first, produces his plunder. It is not extensive. A seal or two, a pencil-case, a pair of sleeve-buttons, and a brooch of no great value, are all. They are severally examined and appraised by old Joe, who chalks the sums he is disposed to give for each upon the wall, and adds them up into a total when he finds there is nothing more to come.

OLD JOE
That's your account, and I wouldn't give another sixpence, if I was to be boiled for not doing it. Who's next?

Mrs. Dilber is next. Sheets and towels, a little wearing apparel, two old-fashioned silver teaspoons, a pair of sugar-tongs, and a few boots. Her account is stated on the wall in the same manner.

OLD JOE
I always give too much to ladies. It's a weakness of mine, and that's the way I ruin myself. That's your account. If you asked me for another penny, and made it an open question, I'd repent of being so liberal and knock off half-a-crown.

CHARWOMAN
And now undo my bundle, Joe.

Joe goes down on his knees for the greater convenience of opening it, and having unfastened a great many knots, drags out a large and heavy roll of some dark stuff.

OLD JOE
What do you call this? Bed-curtains!

CHARWOMAN
Ah! Bed-curtains!

OLD JOE
You don't mean to say you took 'em down, rings and all, with him lying there?

CHARWOMAN
Yes I do. Why not?

OLD JOE
You were born to make your fortune, and you'll certainly do it.

CHARWOMAN
I certainly shan't hold my hand, when I can get anything in it by reaching it out, for the sake of such a man as He was, I promise you, Joe. Don't drop that oil upon the blankets, now.

OLD JOE
His blankets?

CHARWOMAN
Whose else's do you think? He isn't likely to take cold without 'em, I dare say.

OLD JOE
I hope he didn't die of anything catching? Eh?

OLD JOE
(stopping in his work, looking up)

CHARWOMAN
Don't you be afraid of that. I an't so fond of his company that I'd loiter about him for such things, if he did. Ah! You may look through that shirt till your eyes ache; but you won't find a hole in it, nor a threadbare place. It's the best he had, and a fine one too. They'd have wasted it, if it hadn't been for me.

OLD JOE
What do you call wasting of it?

CHARWOMAN
Putting it on him to be buried in, to be sure. Somebody was fool enough to do it, but I took it off again. If calico an't good enough for such a purpose, it isn't good enough for anything. It's quite as becoming to the body. He can't look uglier than he did in that one.

Scrooge listens to this dialogue in horror. As they sit grouped about their spoil, in the scanty light afforded by the old man's lamp, he views them with a detestation and disgust which could hardly have been greater, though they had been obscene demons marketing the corpse itself.

CHARWOMAN
Ha, ha! This is the end of it, you see! He frightened every one away from him when he was alive, to profit us when he was dead! Ha, ha, ha!

SCROOGE
Spirit! I see, I see. The case of this unhappy man might be my own. My life tends that way, now. Merciful Heaven, what is this!

He recoils in terror, for the scene has changed, and now he almost touches a bed: a bare, uncurtained bed: on which, beneath a ragged sheet, there lies a something covered up, which, though it is dumb, announces itself in awful language.

INT. DARK BARE ROOM WITH UNCURTAINED BED - NIGHT

The room is very dark, too dark to be observed with any accuracy, though Scrooge glances round it in obedience to a secret impulse, anxious to know what kind of room it is. A pale light, rising in the outer air, falls straight upon the bed; and on it, plundered and bereft, unwatched, unwept, uncared for, is the body of this man.

(SOUND: cat tearing at door, gnawing rats beneath hearth-stone)

Scrooge glances towards the Phantom. Its steady hand is pointed to the head. The cover is so carelessly adjusted that the slightest raising of it, the motion of a finger upon Scrooge's part, would have disclosed the face. He thinks of it, feels how easy it would be to do, and longs to do it; but has no more power to withdraw the veil than to dismiss the spectre at his side.

Oh cold, cold, rigid, dreadful Death, set up thine altar here, and dress it with such terrors as thou hast at thy command: for this is thy dominion! But of the loved, revered, and honoured head, thou canst not turn one hair to thy dread purposes, or make one feature odious. It is not that the hand is heavy and will fall down when released; it is not that the heart and pulse are still; but that the hand WAS open, generous, and true; the heart brave, warm, and tender; and the pulse a man's. Strike, Shadow, strike! And see his good deeds springing from the wound, to sow the world with life immortal!

No voice pronounces these words in Scrooge's ears, and yet he hears them when he looks upon the bed. He thinks, if this man could be raised up now, what would be his foremost thoughts? Avarice, hard-dealing, griping cares? They have brought him to a rich end, truly!

He lies, in the dark empty house, with not a man, a woman, or a child, to say that he was kind to me in this or that, and for the memory of one kind word I will be kind to him. A cat is tearing at the door, and there is a sound of gnawing rats beneath the hearth-stone. What they wanted in the room of death, and why they were so restless and disturbed, Scrooge does not dare to think.

SCROOGE
Spirit! This is a fearful place. In leaving it, I shall not leave its lesson, trust me. Let us go!

Still the Ghost points with an unmoved finger to the head.

SCROOGE
I understand you, and I would do it, if I could. But I have not the power, Spirit. I have not the power.

Again it seems to look upon him.

SCROOGE
If there is any person in the town who feels emotion caused by this man's death, show that person to me, Spirit, I beseech you!

The Phantom spreads its dark robe before him for a moment, like a wing; and withdrawing it, reveals a room by daylight, where a mother and her children are.

INT. DEBTORS' MODEST HOME (CAROLINE) - DAY

She is expecting some one, and with anxious eagerness; for she walks up and down the room; starts at every sound; looks out from the window; glances at the clock; tries, but in vain, to work with her needle; and can hardly bear the voices of the children in their play.

At length the long-expected knock is heard. She hurries to the door, and meets her husband; a man whose face is careworn and depressed, though he is young. There is a remarkable expression in it now; a kind of serious delight of which he feels ashamed, and which he struggles to repress.

He sits down to the dinner that has been hoarding for him by the fire; and when she asks him faintly what news (which is not until after a long silence), he appears embarrassed how to answer.

CAROLINE
Is it good? Or bad?—to help him.

HUSBAND
Bad.

CAROLINE
We are quite ruined?

HUSBAND
No. There is hope yet, Caroline.

CAROLINE
If he relents, there is! Nothing is past hope, if such a miracle has happened.

HUSBAND
He is past relenting. He is dead.

She is a mild and patient creature if her face spoke truth; but she is thankful in her soul to hear it, and she says so, with clasped hands. She prays forgiveness the next moment, and is sorry; but the first was the emotion of her heart.

HUSBAND
What the half-drunken woman whom I told you of last night said to me, when I tried to see him and obtain a week's delay; and what I thought was a mere excuse to avoid me; turns out to have been quite true. He was not only very ill, but dying, then.

CAROLINE
To whom will our debt be transferred?

HUSBAND
I don't know. But before that time we shall be ready with the money; and even though we were not, it would be a bad fortune indeed to find so merciless a creditor in his successor. We may sleep to-night with light hearts, Caroline!

Yes. Soften it as they would, their hearts are lighter. The children's faces, hushed and clustered round to hear what they so little understood, are brighter; and it is a happier house for this man's death! The only emotion that the Ghost could show him, caused by the event, was one of pleasure.

(SOUND: children's hushed voices, lighter breath)

SCROOGE
Let me see some tenderness connected with a death, or that dark chamber, Spirit, which we left just now, will be for ever present to me.

The Ghost conducts him through several streets familiar to his feet; and as they go along, Scrooge looks here and there to find himself, but nowhere is he to be seen. They enter poor Bob Cratchit's house; the dwelling he had visited before; and find the mother and the children seated round the fire.

INT. BOB CRATCHIT'S FOUR-ROOMED HOUSE IN CAMDEN TOWN - DAY

Quiet. Very quiet. The noisy little Cratchits are as still as statues in one corner, and sit looking up at Peter, who has a book before him. The mother and her daughters are engaged in sewing. But surely they are very quiet!

PETER
(reading)
And He took a child, and set him in the midst of them.

Where had Scrooge heard those words? He had not dreamed them. The boy must have read them out as he and the Spirit crossed the threshold. Why did he not go on?

The mother lays her work upon the table, and puts her hand up to her face.

MRS. CRATCHIT
The colour hurts my eyes.

The colour? Ah, poor Tiny Tim!

MRS. CRATCHIT
They're better now again. It makes them weak by candle-light; and I wouldn't show weak eyes to your father when he comes home, for the world. It must be near his time.

PETER
Past it rather. But I think he has walked a little slower than he used, these few last evenings, mother.

They are very quiet again. At last she says, and in a steady, cheerful voice that only falters once:

MRS. CRATCHIT
I have known him walk with—I have known him walk with Tiny Tim upon his shoulder, very fast indeed.

PETER
And so have I. Often.

OTHER CHILDREN
And so have I.

MRS. CRATCHIT
But he was very light to carry, and his father loved him so, that it was no trouble: no trouble. And there is your father at the door!

She hurries out to meet him; and little Bob in his comforter—he had need of it, poor fellow—comes in. His tea is ready for him on the hob, and they all try who should help him to it most. Then the two young Cratchits get upon his knees and lay each child a little cheek against his face, as if they said, "Don't mind it, father. Don't be grieved!"

Bob is very cheerful with them, and speaks pleasantly to all the family. He looks at the work upon the table, and praises the industry and speed of Mrs. Cratchit and the girls. They would be done long before Sunday, he said.

MRS. CRATCHIT
Sunday! You went to-day, then, Robert?

BOB CRATCHIT
Yes, my dear. I wish you could have gone. It would have done you good to see how green a place it is. But you'll see it often. I promised him that I would walk there on a Sunday. My little, little child!

He breaks down all at once. He couldn't help it. If he could have helped it, he and his child would have been farther apart perhaps than they were.

He leaves the room, and goes up-stairs into the room above, which is lighted cheerfully, and hung with Christmas. There is a chair set close beside the child, and there are signs of some one having been there, lately. Poor Bob sits down in it, and when he has thought a little and composed himself, he kisses the little face. He is reconciled to what has happened, and goes down again quite happy.

They draw about the fire, and talk; the girls and mother working still. Bob tells them of the extraordinary kindness of Mr. Scrooge's nephew, whom he had scarcely seen but once, and who, meeting him in the street that day, and seeing that he looked a little—"just a little down you know," said Bob, inquired what had happened to distress him. "On which," said Bob, "for he is the pleasantest-spoken gentleman you ever heard, I told him. 'I am heartily sorry for it, Mr. Cratchit,' he said, 'and heartily sorry for your good wife.' By the bye, how he ever knew that, I don't know."

MRS. CRATCHIT
Knew what, my dear?

BOB CRATCHIT
Why, that you were a good wife.

PETER
Everybody knows that!

BOB CRATCHIT
Very well observed, my boy! I hope they do. 'Heartily sorry,' he said, 'for your good wife. If I can be of service to you in any way,' he said, giving me his card, 'that's where I live. Pray come to me.'

BOB CRATCHIT
Now, it wasn't for the sake of anything he might be able to do for us, so much as for his kind way, that this was quite delightful. It really seemed as if he had known our Tiny Tim, and felt with us.

MRS. CRATCHIT
I'm sure he's a good soul!

BOB CRATCHIT
You would be surer of it, my dear, if you saw and spoke to him. I shouldn't be at all surprised—mark what I say!—if he got Peter a better situation.

MRS. CRATCHIT
Only hear that, Peter.

GIRL CRATCHIT
And then Peter will be keeping company with some one, and setting up for himself.

PETER
Get along with you!

BOB CRATCHIT
It's just as likely as not, one of these days; though there's plenty of time for that, my dear. But however and whenever we part from one another, I am sure we shall none of us forget poor Tiny Tim—shall we—or this first parting that there was among us?

ALL
Never, father!

BOB CRATCHIT
And I know, my dears, that when we recollect how patient and how mild he was; although he was a little, little child; we shall not quarrel easily among ourselves, and forget poor Tiny Tim in doing it.

ALL
No, never, father!

BOB CRATCHIT
I am very happy. I am very happy!

Mrs. Cratchit kisses him, his daughters kiss him, the two young Cratchits kiss him, and Peter and himself shake hands. Spirit of Tiny Tim, thy childish essence was from God!

(SOUND: quiet fire, soft kisses, Bob's broken happy breath)

SCROOGE
Spectre, something informs me that our parting moment is at hand. I know it, but I know not how. Tell me what man that was whom we saw lying dead?

The Ghost of Christmas Yet To Come conveys him, as before—though at a different time, he thought: indeed, there seemed no order in these latter visions, save that they were in the Future—into the resorts of business men, but shows him not himself. Indeed, the Spirit does not stay for anything, but goes straight on, as to the end just now desired, until besought by Scrooge to tarry for a moment.

SCROOGE
This court through which we hurry now is where my place of occupation is, and has been for a length of time. I see the house. Let me behold what I shall be, in days to come!

The Spirit stops; the hand is pointed elsewhere.

SCROOGE
The house is yonder. Why do you point away?

The inexorable finger undergoes no change.

Scrooge hastens to the window of his office, and looks in. It is an office still, but not his. The furniture is not the same, and the figure in the chair is not himself. The Phantom points as before.

He joins it once again, and wondering why and whither he had gone, accompanies it until they reach an iron gate. He pauses to look round before entering.

EXT. CHURCHYARD WITH NEGLECTED GRAVE - DAY

A churchyard. Here, then; the wretched man whose name he had now to learn, lay underneath the ground. It was a worthy place. Walled in by houses; overrun by grass and weeds, the growth of vegetation's death, not life; choked up with too much burying; fat with repleted appetite. A worthy place!

The Spirit stands among the graves, and points down to One. He advances towards it trembling. The Phantom is exactly as it had been, but he dreads that he saw new meaning in its solemn shape.

(SOUND: wind in weeds, distant city, Scrooge's trembling breath)

SCROOGE
Before I draw nearer to that stone to which you point, answer me one question. Are these the shadows of the things that Will be, or are they shadows of things that May be, only?

Still the Ghost points downward to the grave by which it stands.

SCROOGE
Men's courses will foreshadow certain ends, to which, if persevered in, they must lead. But if the courses be departed from, the ends will change. Say it is thus with what you show me!

The Spirit is immovable as ever.

Scrooge creeps towards it, trembling as he goes; and following the finger, reads upon the stone of the neglected grave his own name, EBENEZER SCROOGE.

SCROOGE
Am I that man who lay upon the bed?

The finger points from the grave to him, and back again.

SCROOGE
No, Spirit! Oh no, no!

The finger still is there.

SCROOGE
Spirit! Hear me! I am not the man I was. I will not be the man I must have been but for this intercourse. Why show me this, if I am past all hope!

For the first time the hand appears to shake.

SCROOGE
Good Spirit, your nature intercedes for me, and pities me. Assure me that I yet may change these shadows you have shown me, by an altered life!

The kind hand trembles.

SCROOGE
I will honour Christmas in my heart, and try to keep it all the year. I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future. The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me. I will not shut out the lessons that they teach. Oh, tell me I may sponge away the writing on this stone!

In his agony, he catches the spectral hand. It seeks to free itself, but he is strong in his entreaty, and detains it. The Spirit, stronger yet, repulses him.

Holding up his hands in a last prayer to have his fate reversed, he sees an alteration in the Phantom's hood and dress. It shrinks, collapses, and dwindles down into a bedpost.

(SOUND: bedpost solidifying, Scrooge's sob of relief)

INT. SCROOGE'S GLOOMY CHAMBERS - DAY

Yes! And the bedpost was his own. The bed was his own, the room was his own. Best and happiest of all, the Time before him was his own, to make amends in!

SCROOGE
I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future! The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me. Oh Jacob Marley! Heaven, and the Christmas Time be praised for this! I say it on my knees, old Jacob; on my knees!

He is so fluttered and so glowing with his good intentions that his broken voice would scarcely answer to his call. He has been sobbing violently in his conflict with the Spirit, and his face is wet with tears.

SCROOGE
They are not torn down, they are not torn down, rings and all. They are here—I am here—the shadows of the things that would have been, may be dispelled. They will be. I know they will!

His hands are busy with his garments all this time; turning them inside out, putting them on upside down, tearing them, mislaying them, making them parties to every kind of extravagance.

SCROOGE
I don't know what to do! I am as light as a feather, I am as happy as an angel, I am as merry as a schoolboy. I am as giddy as a drunken man. A merry Christmas to everybody! A happy New Year to all the world. Hallo here! Whoop! Hallo!

He has frisked into the sitting-room, and is now standing there: perfectly winded.

SCROOGE
There's the saucepan that the gruel was in! There's the door, by which the Ghost of Jacob Marley entered! There's the corner where the Ghost of Christmas Present sat! There's the window where I saw the wandering Spirits! It's all right, it's all true, it all happened. Ha ha ha!

Really, for a man who had been out of practice for so many years, it was a splendid laugh, a most illustrious laugh. The father of a long, long line of brilliant laughs!

SCROOGE
I don't know what day of the month it is! I don't know how long I've been among the Spirits. I don't know anything. I'm quite a baby. Never mind. I don't care. I'd rather be a baby. Hallo! Whoop! Hallo here!

He is checked in his transports by the churches ringing out the lustiest peals he had ever heard. Clash, clang, hammer; ding, dong, bell. Bell, dong, ding; hammer, clang, clash! Oh, glorious, glorious!

(SOUND: lustiest church peals — clash clang hammer ding dong)

Running to the window, he opens it, and puts out his head. No fog, no mist; clear, bright, jovial, stirring, cold; cold, piping for the blood to dance to; Golden sunlight; Heavenly sky; sweet fresh air; merry bells. Oh, glorious! Glorious!

SCROOGE
What's to-day!

He calls downward to a boy in Sunday clothes, who perhaps has loitered in to look about him.

BOY
EH?

SCROOGE
What's to-day, my fine fellow?

BOY
To-day! Why, CHRISTMAS DAY.

SCROOGE
It's Christmas Day! I haven't missed it. The Spirits have done it all in one night. They can do anything they like. Of course they can. Of course they can. Hallo, my fine fellow!

BOY
Hallo!

SCROOGE
Do you know the Poulterer's, in the next street but one, at the corner?

BOY
I should hope I did.

SCROOGE
An intelligent boy! A remarkable boy! Do you know whether they've sold the prize Turkey that was hanging up there?—Not the little prize Turkey: the big one?

BOY
What, the one as big as me?

SCROOGE
What a delightful boy! It's a pleasure to talk to him. Yes, my buck!

BOY
It's hanging there now.

SCROOGE
Is it? Go and buy it.

BOY
Walk-ER!

SCROOGE
No, no, I am in earnest. Go and buy it, and tell 'em to bring it here, that I may give them the direction where to take it. Come back with the man, and I'll give you a shilling. Come back with him in less than five minutes and I'll give you half-a-crown!

The boy is off like a shot. He must have had a steady hand at a trigger who could have got a shot off half so fast.

SCROOGE
(whispering, rubbing hands, splitting with a laugh)
I'll send it to Bob Cratchit's! He sha'n't know who sends it. It's twice the size of Tiny Tim. Joe Miller never made such a joke as sending it to Bob's will be!

The hand in which he writes the address is not a steady one, but write it he does, somehow, and goes down-stairs to open the street door, ready for the coming of the poulterer's man. As he stands there, waiting his arrival, the knocker catches his eye.

SCROOGE
I shall love it, as long as I live! I scarcely ever looked at it before. What an honest expression it has in its face! It's a wonderful knocker!—Here's the Turkey! Hallo! Whoop! How are you! Merry Christmas!

It was a Turkey! He never could have stood upon his legs, that bird. He would have snapped 'em short off in a minute, like sticks of sealing-wax.

SCROOGE
Why, it's impossible to carry that to Camden Town. You must have a cab.

The chuckle with which he said this, and the chuckle with which he paid for the Turkey, and the chuckle with which he paid for the cab, and the chuckle with which he recompensed the boy, were only to be exceeded by the chuckle with which he sat down breathless in his chair again, and chuckled till he cried.

(SOUND: turkey delivery, cab wheels, Scrooge's continuous chuckle)

Shaving was not an easy task, for his hand continued to shake very much; and shaving requires attention, even when you don't dance while you are at it. But if he had cut the end of his nose off, he would have put a piece of sticking-plaister over it, and been quite satisfied.

He dresses himself all in his best, and at last gets out into the streets. The people are by this time pouring forth, as he had seen them with the Ghost of Christmas Present; and walking with his hands behind him, Scrooge regards every one with a delighted smile. He looks so irresistibly pleasant, in a word, that three or four good-humoured fellows say, "Good morning, sir! A merry Christmas to you!" And Scrooge said often afterwards, that of all the blithe sounds he had ever heard, those were the blithest in his ears.

EXT. LONDON STREETS AND COURT ON CHRISTMAS EVE - DAY

He has not gone far, when coming on towards him he beholds the portly gentleman who had walked into his counting-house the day before and said, "Scrooge and Marley's, I believe?" It sends a pang across his heart to think how this old gentleman would look upon him when they met; but he knows what path lay straight before him, and he takes it.

SCROOGE
My dear sir, how do you do? I hope you succeeded yesterday. It was very kind of you. A merry Christmas to you, sir!

PORTLY GENTLEMAN 1
Mr. Scrooge?

SCROOGE
Yes. That is my name, and I fear it may not be pleasant to you. Allow me to ask your pardon. And will you have the goodness—

Here Scrooge whispers in his ear.

PORTLY GENTLEMAN 1
Lord bless me! My dear Mr. Scrooge, are you serious?

SCROOGE
If you please. Not a farthing less. A great many back-payments are included in it, I assure you. Will you do me that favour?

PORTLY GENTLEMAN 1
My dear sir, I don't know what to say to such munifi—

SCROOGE
Don't say anything, please. Come and see me. Will you come and see me?

PORTLY GENTLEMAN 1
I will!

And it was clear he meant to do it.

SCROOGE
Thank'ee. I am much obliged to you. I thank you fifty times. Bless you!

He goes to church, and walks about the streets, and watches the people hurrying to and fro, and pats children on the head, and questions beggars, and looks down into the kitchens of houses, and up to the windows, and finds that everything could yield him pleasure. He had never dreamed that any walk—that anything—could give him so much happiness. In the afternoon he turns his steps towards his nephew's house.

INT. FRED'S HOUSE (CHRISTMAS DAY VISIT) - DAY

He passes the door a dozen times, before he has the courage to go up and knock. But he makes a dash, and does it.

SCROOGE
Is your master at home, my dear?

NICE GIRL
Yes, sir.

SCROOGE
Where is he, my love?

NICE GIRL
He's in the dining-room, sir, along with mistress. I'll show you up-stairs, if you please.

SCROOGE
Thank'ee. He knows me. I'll go in here, my dear.

He turns it gently, and sidles his face in, round the door. They are looking at the table (which is spread out in great array); for these young housekeepers are always nervous on such points, and like to see that everything is right.

SCROOGE
Fred!

Dear heart alive, how his niece by marriage started! Scrooge had forgotten, for the moment, about her sitting in the corner with the footstool, or he wouldn't have done it, on any account.

FRED
Why bless my soul! Who's that?

SCROOGE
It's I. Your uncle Scrooge. I have come to dinner. Will you let me in, Fred?

Let him in! It is a mercy he didn't shake his arm off. He was at home in five minutes. Nothing could be heartier. His niece looked just the same. So did Topper when he came. So did the plump sister when she came. So did every one when they came. Wonderful party, wonderful games, wonderful unanimity, won-der-ful happiness!

(SOUND: hearty welcome, laughter, party rising)

INT. SCROOGE'S OFFICE NEXT MORNING - DAY

But he was early at the office next morning. Oh, he was early there. If he could only be there first, and catch Bob Cratchit coming late! That was the thing he had set his heart upon.

And he did it; yes, he did! The clock struck nine. No Bob. A quarter past. No Bob. He was full eighteen minutes and a half behind his time. Scrooge sat with his door wide open, that he might see him come into the Tank.

(SOUND: clock striking nine, then quarter past)

His hat was off, before he opened the door; his comforter too. He was on his stool in a jiffy; driving away with his pen, as if he were trying to overtake nine o'clock.

SCROOGE
(in his accustomed voice, as near as he could feign it)
Hallo! What do you mean by coming here at this time of day?

BOB CRATCHIT
I am very sorry, sir. I am behind my time.

SCROOGE
You are? Yes. I think you are. Step this way, sir, if you please.

BOB CRATCHIT
It's only once a year, sir. It shall not be repeated. I was making rather merry yesterday, sir.

SCROOGE
Now, I'll tell you what, my friend. I am not going to stand this sort of thing any longer. And therefore—

He continues, leaping from his stool, and giving Bob such a dig in the waistcoat that he staggered back into the Tank again—

SCROOGE
and therefore I am about to raise your salary!

Bob trembled, and got a little nearer to the ruler. He had a momentary idea of knocking Scrooge down with it, holding him, and calling to the people in the court for help and a strait-waistcoat.

SCROOGE
A merry Christmas, Bob! A merrier Christmas, Bob, my good fellow, than I have given you, for many a year! I'll raise your salary, and endeavour to assist your struggling family, and we will discuss your affairs this very afternoon, over a Christmas bowl of smoking bishop, Bob! Make up the fires, and buy another coal-scuttle before you dot another i, Bob Cratchit!

(SOUND: Scrooge's clap on Bob's back, Bob's astonished breath, fires to be made up)

Scrooge was better than his word. He did it all, and infinitely more; and to Tiny Tim, who did NOT die, he was a second father. He became as good a friend, as good a master, and as good a man, as the good old city knew, or any other good old city, town, or borough, in the good old world. Some people laughed to see the alteration in him, but he let them laugh, and little heeded them; for he was wise enough to know that nothing ever happened on this globe, for good, at which some people did not have their fill of laughter in the outset; and knowing that such as these would be blind anyway, he thought it quite as well that they should wrinkle up their eyes in grins, as have the malady in less attractive forms. His own heart laughed: and that was quite enough for him.

He had no further intercourse with Spirits, but lived upon the Total Abstinence Principle, ever afterwards; and it was always said of him, that he knew how to keep Christmas well, if any man alive possessed the knowledge. May that be truly said of us, and all of us! And so, as Tiny Tim observed, God bless Us, Every One!

(SOUND: distant happy church bells, children's laughter, a heart at peace)

FADE OUT.

THE END

